---
layout: base.njk
title: Churches
description: Churches serving the Lenches, Harvington and Abbots Morton — the ARCH Benefice and its service times, plus Unitarian and Methodist congregations in Evesham.
---
<style>
  /* Service times — single column of date cards, stacked list inside */
  .svc-col{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;max-width:680px}
  .svc-col .card{margin:0}
  .svc-list{margin:.5rem 0 0}
  .svc{padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}
  .svc:last-child{border-bottom:0;padding-bottom:0}
  .svc-head{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.1rem .6rem;align-items:baseline}
  .svc-time{font-weight:700;color:var(--green-dark);white-space:nowrap}
  .svc-church{font-weight:600}
  .svc-detail{margin:.1rem 0 0}
</style>
<main class="container">

  {%- set churchNames = { "Rous Lench": "St Peter's, Rous Lench", "Church Lench": "All Saints', Church Lench", "Harvington": "St James the Great, Harvington", "Abbots Morton": "St Peter's, Abbots Morton", "Norton": "St Egwin's, Norton" } %}

  <div class="page-head" id="arch">
    <h1>Churches</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">The Lenches and their neighbours are served by the ARCH Benefice, named after the parishes of <strong>A</strong>bbots Morton, <strong>R</strong>ous Lench, <strong>C</strong>hurch Lench and <strong>H</strong>arvington, which were brought together in 2001 within the Diocese of Worcester. Its churches are All Saints', Church Lench; St Peter's, Rous Lench; St James the Great, Harvington; St Peter's, Abbots Morton; and St Egwin's at Norton &amp; Lenchwick, which shares the rota though the parish formally sits in the Evesham benefice. Several other congregations are a short drive away in Evesham.</p>
    <p>All Saints' stands at the top of the hill with one of the best views in Worcestershire, looking west towards the Malverns. St Peter's at Rous Lench is unusual in having two pulpits and no fixed pews, so the space can be used flexibly.</p>
    <p class="small"><a href="https://www.archbenefice.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ARCH Benefice website &rarr;</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.archbenefice.org.uk/information/arch-messenger" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the ARCH Messenger &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  {#- Photos come from src/_data/photos.json (Photos tab, target "churches"). Caption = church name by village. -#}
  <div class="gallery">
  {%- for p in photos %}{% if "churches" in p.targets %}
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <a href="{{ p.src | url }}" data-viewer data-caption="{{ p.alt | escape }}"><img src="{{ p.src | url }}" alt="{{ p.alt | escape }}" width="{{ p.width }}" height="{{ p.height }}" loading="lazy"{% if p.height > p.width %} style="object-position:50% 20%"{% endif %}></a>
      <figcaption class="small muted">{{ churchNames[p.village] or p.village }}{% if p.credit %} &middot; {{ p.credit | escape }}{% endif %}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
  </div>

  {#- Service times come from src/_data/services.json (typed from the ARCH Messenger rota). Past dates drop off at the nightly rebuild. -#}
  <section id="services">
    <h2>Service times</h2>
    <p>Services rotate around the churches, so the pattern changes from week to week. {{ services.midweek }} <span class="small muted">From the {{ services.source }}.</span></p>

    {%- set svc = services.dates | upcoming %}
    {%- if svc.length %}
    {%- for g in svc | byMonth %}
    <h3>{{ g.label }}</h3>
    <div class="svc-col">
      {%- for d in g.items %}
      <div class="card">
        <h4 class="mt0" style="margin-bottom:0.15rem">{{ d.date | longDate }}</h4>
        {% if d.name %}<p class="small muted mt0">{{ d.name }}</p>{% endif %}
        <div class="svc-list">
          {%- for s in d.services %}
          <div class="svc">
            <div class="svc-head"><span class="svc-time">{{ s.time }}</span> <span class="svc-church">{{ churchNames[s.village] or s.village }}</span></div>
            <p class="svc-detail">{{ s.service }}{% if s.note %} <span class="small muted">({{ s.note }})</span>{% endif %}</p>
          </div>
          {%- endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- endfor %}
    {%- else %}
    <p>No service times are listed for the coming weeks yet; please check the ARCH Messenger.</p>
    {%- endif %}
  </section>

  <section id="evesham">
    <h2>Other congregations in Evesham</h2>
    <div class="grid">

      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="mt0">Evesham Unitarians</h3>
        <p class="mt0">Oat Street Chapel, Oat Street, Evesham WR11 4PJ. Sunday gatherings at 11am. There is a hearing loop and an accessible toilet, and the chapel conducts same-sex weddings.</p>
        <p>The building is a genuine curiosity — a remarkable survivor tucked back from the street behind the modern town centre. The hall is hired out to groups promoting dignity and respect for all, regardless of belief, gender, ability or sexual orientation.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="mt0">Evesham Methodist Church</h3>
        <p class="mt0">71 Bridge Street, Evesham WR11 4SF &middot; 01386 442380. Sunday morning worship at 10.30am, with a midweek communion on Wednesdays and a shoppers' service on Fridays.</p>
        <p>Part of the South Worcestershire Methodist Circuit, and recognised as a Church of Sanctuary.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="differences">
    <h2>How Methodists and Unitarians differ from the Church of England</h2>

    <p><strong>Methodism</strong> grew out of the Church of England rather than against it. John Wesley lived and died an Anglican priest, and the separation only came after his death. Doctrinally the two remain close; the real differences are structural. There are no bishops — authority sits with the annual Conference and the local circuit, which is why one minister covers several churches and lay preachers lead many services. Methodism has historically been strong on personal conversion, hymn-singing and social action, and was traditionally teetotal, which is why you will often find non-alcoholic communion wine.</p>

    <p><strong>Unitarians</strong> are a different proposition altogether. They descend from English Dissenters who rejected the doctrine of the Trinity — hence the name — and they hold no binding creed. Members are not required to affirm any particular belief about God or Jesus, and congregations today include Christians, humanists and people drawing on other traditions. Each congregation governs itself entirely. Unitarians were legally persecuted until the Doctrine of the Trinity Act of 1813, which is part of why chapels like Oat Street are often tucked down side streets rather than facing the market square.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="accuracy">
    <div class="notice">
      <p class="small" style="margin:0">These details are gathered from published sources and may not be complete. We'd welcome hearing directly from the churches so we can keep this page accurate — please email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
